WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.310
The exam office has a thousand

2
00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:06.390
candidates in a sorted list, and a

3
00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:08.510
student asks: am I on it?

4
00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:11.930
Checking every name could

5
00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:13.730
take a thousand looks.

6
00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:16.820
There’s a way that needs only ten.

7
00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.440
Today: searching, one by

8
00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:22.040
one or by halving.

9
00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:25.390
Linear search checks each item

10
00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:27.050
in turn, from the start.

11
00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:30.650
It works on any list, sorted or not.

12
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.580
If the item is found, return its

13
00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:36.200
position; if the loop ends without

14
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.500
finding it, return minus one.

15
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:41.060
How much work is that?

16
00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:43.910
If the item is last, or missing,

17
00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:46.650
linear search looks at every item.

18
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.300
For a thousand names, that’s a

19
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:50.020
thousand comparisons.

20
00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:53.140
For a million, a million.

21
00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.060
The work grows in step with the list.

22
00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.880
Binary search is cleverer, but

23
00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.240
it needs a sorted list.

24
00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:03.310
Look at the middle item.

25
00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:05.930
If it’s what you want, stop.

26
00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:09.340
If your target is bigger, throw away the

27
00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:12.240
lower half; if smaller, the upper half.

28
00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:14.450
Repeat on what’s left.

29
00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:17.870
Each step halves what’s left.

30
00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:21.330
A thousand, five hundred, two

31
00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:23.330
hundred and fifty, and so on:

32
00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:25.810
about ten steps to reach one.

33
00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:28.810
A million needs only about twenty.

34
00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.320
That’s why dictionaries and

35
00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.340
contact lists are kept in order.

36
00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:35.000
Quick check!

37
00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.200
Which list can binary search use: the

38
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.440
names in the order pupils arrived, or

39
00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.120
the names in alphabetical order?

40
00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:44.670
The alphabetical list.

41
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.680
Binary search throws half away on

42
00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.020
every step, and that’s only safe

43
00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:50.960
if the list is sorted.

44
00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:54.200
On an unsorted list, it can miss a

45
00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:55.340
name that’s really there.

46
00:01:56.550 --> 00:02:00.070
In code, keep two markers, lo and hi,

47
00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:01.370
around the part that’s left.

48
00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:04.100
Each time round, look at the middle.

49
00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:07.490
Move lo up or hi down.

50
00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:11.390
When lo passes hi, the target isn’t there.

51
00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:14.360
So which should you use?

52
00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.370
For a short list, or one that isn’t

53
00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:20.410
sorted, linear search is simple and fine.

54
00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:23.970
For a big sorted list searched again and

55
00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.550
again, binary search wins by miles.

56
00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:30.830
Sorting first costs time, so it pays

57
00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:32.370
only when you search often.

58
00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:35.990
In the app, open Guess the number

59
00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:38.170
and choose one to a thousand.

60
00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:41.890
Race one by one against halving, and watch

61
00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:43.990
halving finish in about ten steps.

62
00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.790
Then read the table: a

63
00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:48.610
million adds only ten more.

64
00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:50.590
Your turn.

65
00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:54.830
A sorted list has a thousand

66
00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:56.070
and twenty-four names.

67
00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.480
At most, how many looks does

68
00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:00.860
binary search need?

69
00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:02.890
About eleven.

70
00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:06.880
Remember: linear search checks every

71
00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.160
item, and works on any list.

72
00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:11.870
Binary search halves what’s left,

73
00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:13.670
but needs a sorted list.

74
00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.460
For a thousand items, a thousand

75
00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:18.300
looks against about ten.

76
00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:22.870
Try it yourself: take a dictionary or a

77
00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:25.610
phone’s contact list, and find a word

78
00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:28.670
by halving: open the middle, then keep

79
00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:30.070
the half it must be in.

80
00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:31.370
Count your looks.

81
00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:33.930
Then try one by one!
